`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
This task is a small but instructive example of the "shotgun surgery" a single new field can trigger when a codebase talks to the same external API via two different transports. GitLab's REST and GraphQL APIs are semantically the same data, but this repo models them as two entirely separate wire-format structs (`gl.MergeRequest` from the vendored go-gitlab library vs. the hand-rolled `GQLMergeRequest` + raw query-string literals in `pkg/gitlab/graphql.go`) that only converge once they reach the mapper functions and produce a single `domain.MergeRequest`. That convergence point is exactly why `internal/domain` is stdlib-only per the repo's non-negotiable rules — it's the one place vendor differences disappear.

Also worth noting: GraphQL query strings in Go are just string literals, so the compiler can't catch a missing field the way it would catch a missing struct field — `sourceBranch`/`targetBranch` had to be added to the query text in two separate places (`gqlUserMRsQuery` and `gqlReviewerMRsQuery`) or the server would silently omit that data from the response, leaving the corresponding Go struct field permanently zero-valued with no compile or runtime error.
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`

